struct payload
{
    /// JSON DOM.
    json_value model{};

    /// Writer serialization buffer (max size, allocated on write).
    mutable http::flat_buffer_ptr buffer{};
};

text:
The proxy is stateless and unconfigured, so the caller (channel) retains control over read buffering options via the passed buffer, and `json` serialization (which also requires a buffer) buffer control is maintained by its exposure on the http::json::body object, accessible to the caller as `response::body().buffer`. In both cases this allows the channel to reuse a single buffer for all reads and for json writes for a given channel instance. So dynamic allocation can be eliminated, allowing the buffers to remain stack allocated. If the `json` serialization buffer is not provided, it will get allocated at a hardwired size internally. The `payload` object here is provided by the `boost::beast` construction of the request/response as the `.body()`.
```struct payload
{
    /// JSON DOM.
    json_value model{};

    /// Writer serialization buffer (max size, allocated on write).
    mutable http::flat_buffer_ptr buffer{};
};```

